// Blocked NON-divisible schedule: one worker, tiling Pass 3's
// within-block-scan ACCUMULATION axis `j` with a block size that
// does NOT divide its range. This is the TASK-0173 AC#3 proof.
//
// Same placement as the naive schedule (single `host`, all five
// kernels on it). The only difference is `loop j : block=6;`, which
// tiles Pass 3's innermost `for j : 0 .. BS` loop. Per PRD §6.3.3
// `block=` is orthogonal to worker placement; a single-worker blocked
// schedule exercises ONLY the tiling transform (TASK-0030's
// `apply_block_transforms`) plus the per-occurrence absolute-index
// rebinding the backend reads from `Event::Loop.block_tag`
// (TASK-0180).
//
// Why `loop j` and `block=6` — DELIBERATELY NON-DIVISIBLE
// (this is the TASK-0173 AC#3 accumulator proof)
// --------------------------------------------------------
// This INVERTS the rationale documented in the sibling
// `blocked.sched.nuc` (and in 06-separable-filter/blocked, which
// stays DELIBERATELY DIVISIBLE on purpose). Those pick a divisor so
// no remainder tile is ever produced. This schedule does the
// opposite, on purpose, to close TASK-0173 AC#3:
//
//   "A synthetic non-divisible blocked schedule over an ACCUMULATOR
//    kernel produces bit-identical output to its naive schedule."
//
// The variable `j` is Pass 3's innermost loop:
//
//     for b : 0 .. NB { for i : 0 .. BS { for j : 0 .. BS {
//         out[b][i] <-- block_scan(out[b][i], in_arr[b][j],
//                                  block_off[b], i, j);
//     } } }
//
// Three properties make this the strongest possible AC#3 witness:
//
//   1. `j` is a GENUINE ACCUMULATOR axis, NOT idempotent. Each step
//      reads `out[b][i]` and writes `out[b][i]` back — an additive
//      fold over `j` (block_scan adds `in_arr[b][j]` iff `j <= i`,
//      and the block offset once at `j == 0`). This is exactly the
//      non-idempotent shape 05-stencil's `blur3` is NOT (blur3 writes
//      `img_out = f(img_in)` and never reads its own output, so it
//      survives gross re-recomputation by accident). If the absolute
//      `j` rebinding were wrong on ANY tile, the `j <= i` / `j == 0`
//      predicates would select the wrong input terms and the running
//      sum would diverge — a wrong byte, not a silently-absorbed one.
//
//   2. `j` is the ACTUAL reduction axis being tiled (not an
//      independent partition axis like `b` in the sibling schedule,
//      where blocks are independent and tiling only reorders them).
//      Tiling the reduction axis itself is the case most sensitive
//      to a base-offset bug.
//
//   3. `j` occurs EXACTLY ONCE in the algorithm (only Pass 3). This
//      isolates the non-divisible accumulator path cleanly: there is
//      no reused-name interaction (the TASK-0180 reused-`b` /
//      count==1 history) confounding the diagnosis — a failure here
//      is unambiguously the non-divisible partial-tile rebinding.
//
// Non-divisibility arithmetic: BS = N / NB = 256 / 4 = 64, so
// `for j : 0 .. BS` has length L = 64. `block=6` does NOT divide 64
// (64 = 6*10 + 4): `apply_block_transforms` rewrites it into a
// FULL-tile nest of `num_full = 10` tiles of width 6 PLUS one
// trailing PARTIAL tile of width `rem = 4` (its tile loop is `0..1`).
// num_full = 10 >= 2 and the remainder 4 is non-zero, so BOTH the
// full-tile nest and the trailing-partial-tile nest are emitted —
// exactly the two-sibling-nest path TASK-0180's per-occurrence
// `block_tag` rebinding covers:
//   - full-tile nest (is_partial = false):
//         abs j = LO + j__tile*N + j   = 0 + j__tile*6 + j
//   - trailing partial tile (is_partial = true):
//         abs j = LO + num_full*N + j  = 0 + 10*6 + j   (= 60 + j)
// The constant `num_full*N` (= 60) base is the whole point: the
// partial tile's own tile loop is `0..1` so `tile*N` would be 0 (the
// WRONG base, 0..4 instead of 60..64). The tag-driven rebinding
// supplies the correct constant base.
//
// AC#3 proof obligation: this cell MUST be byte-identical to the
// hand-written backend-independent `reference.bin` (an independent
// straight-line running sum) on BOTH tier-1 backends — i.e. the
// non-divisible blocked accumulator computes the SAME prefix sums as
// the naive schedule. A green cell here is the AC#3 proof; a wrong
// byte would be a REAL non-divisible-accumulator bug (a valuable
// finding, NOT to be faked or silently skipped).
//
// Honest scope (mirrors 05-stencil / 07-matmul / the sibling
// blocked): single-`host`, so the backend routes the shared
// single-worker renderer; this cell pins the *compile-doesn't-reject
// + passes-don't-panic + bit-identical result* property over a
// non-divisible accumulator. The tiling decomposition's structural
// numeric correctness is additionally pinned by the
// `block_transform` unit tests. Under mp-tcp this is single-process
// (0 used workers ⇒ the shared single-worker renderer ⇒ arithmetic
// byte-identical to pthreads-sync).

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host };

    place load_input    on host;
    place save_output   on host;
    place accumulate    on host;
    place exclusive_add on host;
    place block_scan    on host;

    // Tile Pass 3's within-block-scan ACCUMULATION axis `j` into
    // 6-wide chunks. BS=64 is NOT evenly divisible by 6
    // (64 = 6*10 + 4): exactly num_full=10 full tiles plus ONE
    // trailing partial tile of width 4. This DELIBERATELY produces a
    // remainder tile over a non-idempotent accumulator — the
    // TASK-0173 AC#3 proof that TASK-0180's per-occurrence
    // partial-tile rebinding (LO + num_full*N + inner) is correct.
    // `j` occurs exactly once (Pass 3 only), so this isolates the
    // non-divisible accumulator path with no reused-name confound.
    loop j : block=6;

    // No transfer directives — single worker, no cross-worker edges.
}
